{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"58f2742f3bae28bf","name":"card_event_victory_garden","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/USA/Homefront/events/card_event_victory_garden","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"VICTORY GARDEN","text":"Each US unit in your deck gets: \"Destruction: Give the top unit in your deck +1+1.\"    ","flavorText":"During WWII, the US government urged civilians to plant Victory Gardens to bolster the nation’s food supply. These homegrown plots, often found in backyards, schoolyards and vacant lots, produced vegetables and herbs that supplemented rationed food. By 1943, an estimated 20 million gardens had been planted across the country, yielding nearly 9 million tons of produce. This effort not only alleviated pressure on commercial agriculture but also conserved vital resources like transportation and labor for the military. Victory Gardens became a symbol of civic duty, giving ordinary citizens a tangible way to contribute to the war effort. Government pamphlets, radio broadcasts and local campaigns provided practical gardening advice and encouragement. Children participated through school programs, and families often kept hens alongside their vegetable plots for fresh eggs. These gardens transformed neighborhoods into productive spaces and helped maintain morale during a time of scarcity.","faction":"USA","type":"order","rarity":"Unique","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_victory_garden","title":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_victory_garden_title","SourceString":"VICTORY GARDEN","LocalizedString":"VICTORY GARDEN"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::USA","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_usa","Key":"card_event_victory_garden_text","SourceString":"Each US unit in your deck gets: \"Destruction: Give the top unit in your deck +1+1.\"    ","LocalizedString":"Each US unit in your deck gets: \"Destruction: Give the top unit in y